  "body": "  Let Then is nonempty, the rational localisation is not flat, and multiplication by is a strict inclusion on even though maps to zero in .   Lean (hypothesis on the base field)  Lean (the p-adic finite-jet ring)  Lean (evaluation at W=Q=0)  Lean (the point lies in the chart)  Lean (multiplication by Q² is an isometry)  Lean (Q² restricts to zero)  Lean (Problem 28 witness)  Lean (nonflat completed chart)   The composite is norm-nonincreasing. It therefore sends into , and pulling back the norm valuation on gives a continuous valuation belonging to . At this point has value zero and has nonzero value, so the point lies in .  Since the restricted Gauss norm on is multiplicative and , for every we have Thus multiplication by is an isometry. Its map onto its image, with the subspace topology, is consequently a homeomorphism, so the inclusion is strict. Moreover, an isometric image of the complete space is closed in . Since , the element is nonzero; multiplication by it is therefore injective because is a domain.  On the other hand, the calculation says directly that the canonical map to the completed rational localisation kills . The isomorphism of identifies this localisation with the nonzero ring If the canonical map were flat, the non-zero-divisor would still act injectively on ; equivalently, tensoring the injection with would make multiplication by the image of injective. Its image is zero, however, so this multiplication map is zero on the nonzero ring , a contradiction.   "
